const HERITAGE_SOURCES={
 loan:{title:'Tu Thi Loan — Dong Ho Folk Painting and Protection of Handicraft',publisher:'Vietnam National Institute of Culture and Arts Studies; university-hosted paper',date:'Research predating the 2025 UNESCO inscription',url:'https://chss.nuk.edu.tw/var/file/41/1041/img/981/886004955.pdf'},
 pictorial:{title:'Dong Ho paintings reveal Vietnamese unique folk culture',publisher:'Vietnam Pictorial / Vietnam News Agency',date:'17 October 2019',url:'https://vietnam.vnanet.vn/english/print/dong-ho-paintings-reveal-vietnamese-unique-folk-culture-213147.html'},
 vov:{title:'Dong Ho folk paintings — national cultural heritage',publisher:'Voice of Vietnam, reported interviews',date:'Historical interview account; consulted 5 November 2026',url:'https://vovworld.vn/culture/dong-ho-folk-paintings-national-cultural-heritage-1120332.vov5'},
 materials:{title:'Intangible Cultural Heritage Safeguarding Efforts in Vietnam',publisher:'ICHCAP / UNESCO Category 2 Centre',date:'2015 field survey, Dong Ho entry',url:'https://archive.unesco-ichcap.org/eng/ek/sub1/pdf_file/south_east_asia/2015Vietnam/Field%20Survey%20Report_Vietnam_full.pdf'},
 families:{title:'Dong Ho needs preservation and promotion',publisher:'Nhan Dan',date:'18 May 2024',url:'https://en.nhandan.vn/vietnamese-traditional-folk-painting-genre-dong-ho-needs-preservation-and-promotion-post135690.html'},
 revival:{title:'Reviving the Dong Ho folk painting craft',publisher:'Nhan Dan',date:'Historical reporting, published before the 2025 inscription',url:'https://en.nhandan.vn/reviving-the-dong-ho-folk-painting-craft-post84961.html'},
 gallery:{title:'First private gallery of Dong Ho folk paintings inaugurated in Bac Ninh',publisher:'Vietnam News Agency',date:'19 April 2024',url:'https://vnanet.vn/en/anh/vna-photos-1027/first-private-gallery-of-dong-ho-folk-paintings-inaugurated-in-bac-ninh-7329892.html'},
 unesco:{title:'Decision 20.COM 7.A.1 — Craft of making Dong Ho Folk woodblock printings',publisher:'UNESCO',date:'2025 Intergovernmental Committee decision',url:'https://ich.unesco.org/en/decisions/20.COM/7.A.1'},
 government:{title:'Dong Ho craft inscribed on the Urgent Safeguarding List',publisher:'Vietnam Government News',date:'9 December 2025',url:'https://baochinhphu.vn/nghe-lam-tranh-dan-gian-dong-ho-tro-thanh-di-san-thu-17-cua-viet-nam-duoc-unesco-ghi-danh-1022512091722362.htm'},
 certificate:{title:'Bac Ninh receives the UNESCO certificate',publisher:'Bac Ninh provincial portal',date:'Ceremony held 27 March 2026',url:'https://www.bacninh.gov.vn/web/english/news/-/details/1090513/bac-ninh-province-receives-unesco-certificate-and-opens-coming-back-to-heritage-region-of-bac-ninh-2026-festival-97999444'}
};
const HERITAGE_CHAPTERS=[
 {
  id:'village',period:'Across centuries',short:'The village',title:'A village becomes a printing tradition',image:'village',imageNote:'An illustrated village reconstruction, not a photograph of a documented building.',sources:['loan','government'],kind:'Documented history · origins remain uncertain',
  intro:`Before it became an image in a book or on a screen, Dong Ho was a craft practiced within a village community in Bac Ninh, northern Vietnam. Its history developed through generations of makers, rather than a single recorded founding moment. Designs, tools, and ways of working moved between people as well as across time. To understand the paintings, begin with that community: the place where an image could become a family skill, a seasonal livelihood, and a shared cultural language.`,
  deeper:[
   `How old is the tradition? Different accounts answer that question at different scales. Government reporting describes roughly five centuries of practice, while researcher Tu Thi Loan discusses limited early evidence and a clearer, recognizable style by the seventeenth century. Those accounts should not be compressed into an exact birthday. The distinction is useful: the history of woodblock printing in Vietnam is broader than the history of one village's particular painting tradition.`,
   `Think of a village workshop as a meeting of decisions. A maker chooses a familiar subject, organizes the shapes, prepares the blocks, and judges each impression. Another generation can inherit those tools without repeating every choice in precisely the same way. Continuity does not require every print to be identical.`,
   `This chapter's village drawing is an invitation to imagine that setting, not evidence of what a particular street looked like centuries ago. When you explore the artwork next, ask what knowledge would need to pass from one person's hands to another for a design to survive. The strongest historical question is not only when the craft began, but how people kept it recognizable while making it again.`
  ],reflection:'What survives when a design passes from one generation to the next?',action:{label:'Explore the visual language',href:'ExplorePage.html'}
 },
 {
  id:'new-year',period:'A recurring tradition',short:'Pictures for life',title:'Pictures for a new year',image:'chickens',imageNote:'Hen and Chicks: a contemporary vector study. Symbolic meanings are interpretations.',sources:['pictorial','vov'],kind:'Cultural practice · interpretations vary',
  intro:`A picture could be part of welcoming a new year into the home. Dong Ho prints brought familiar scenes into an atmosphere of hopes, blessings, and renewal. Animals, celebrations, and ordinary work could carry more than one meaning: a family of pigs might suggest abundance, while a hen and chicks could evoke a flourishing household. These associations belong to lived culture. They are ways of reading an image, rather than a dictionary that gives every detail one permanent meaning.`,
  deeper:[
   `Vietnam Pictorial describes the custom of displaying Dong Ho prints during Lunar New Year in the northern delta. That setting changes how a visitor might look at the pictures. A subject that seems modest in an exhibition can be generous in a household: a wish for enough food, a lively family, or a more fortunate year. The print speaks through things people recognize.`,
   `Reported interviews from Voice of Vietnam also show that viewers do not always agree. An image of pigs can be read as a blessing of prosperity, or through a farmer's knowledge of healthy livestock and their care. Neither reading needs to erase the other. Experience helps shape what the viewer notices.`,
   `Try two readings of the hen and chicks. First, simply describe what you can see: a large bird, smaller birds, repeated curves, and a gathering of bodies. Then consider the wish the arrangement might express. Keeping observation separate from interpretation makes the picture more interesting, and helps avoid treating a symbolic explanation as a proven historical event. You can carry that same habit into Mouse Wedding, where a cheerful procession also invites a sharper social reading.`
  ],reflection:'What changes when a painting is a wish displayed at home?',action:{label:'Look for the symbols',href:'ExplorePage.html'}
 },
 {
  id:'materials',period:'Inside the workshop',short:'The making',title:'Paper, shells, plants, and carved wood',image:'materials',imageNote:'A teaching illustration of paper and blocks. Digital layers do not reproduce historical block sets.',sources:['materials','pictorial','government'],kind:'Documented materials · digital process adapted',
  intro:`The paper is part of the artwork before the first image appears. Handmade bark paper receives a coating containing powdered shells, giving the surface its distinctive character. Natural color materials and carved wooden blocks then turn a design into successive impressions. Separate areas of color and the final dark lines must meet on the sheet. What looks like one confident picture is the result of preparation, repeated contact, and skilled decisions about material, order, and placement throughout the process.`,
  deeper:[
   `The 2015 field survey published by ICHCAP describes the special paper and the relationship between color blocks and the outline block. Vietnam Pictorial's workshop reporting shows carving, color preparation, printing, and drying as connected activities. These references help explain why a finished print cannot be understood through its drawing alone. The support and the means of transferring the image also matter.`,
   `Published material accounts include red from mineral sources, blue from indigo, yellow from plant materials, white from shells, and black from burnt plant material. Recipes and tones should be attributed to their particular account, rather than treated as one universal formula. A bright screen swatch is an aid to recognition, not a sample of an artisan's pigment.`,
   `In the digital printing lesson, you can watch color areas arrive separately and see black contours complete the image. The masks were made for this prototype; they are not recovered original woodblocks. Use the lesson to notice relationships: why preparation comes before transfer, why successive impressions need to align, and how a final line can make separate areas suddenly read as a complete figure. Those observations are a beginning for learning from real practice.`
  ],reflection:'Which part of the image belongs to the paper, rather than the ink?',action:{label:'Try the printing lesson',href:'WorkshopPage.html',mode:'print'}
 },
 {
  id:'change',period:'The twentieth century',short:'Changing times',title:'A craft through changing times',image:'blocks',imageNote:'An illustrative arrangement of woodblocks, not surviving objects or documentary evidence.',sources:['loan','revival'],kind:'Historical research · dated accounts',
  intro:`The tradition did not move through the twentieth century without interruption. Research describes damage and disruption during war, later revival and cooperative production, and changing markets that made the craft harder to sustain. Those shifts affected more than the number of pictures being sold. They shaped whether people could earn a living, keep tools in use, and make time to teach. A history of Dong Ho therefore needs to follow working conditions as well as the images themselves.`,
  deeper:[
   `Tu Thi Loan's historical account describes wartime losses of paintings and woodblocks, revival after 1954, and cooperative production serving domestic and overseas markets. It also describes difficulties as markets changed in the 1990s. This is a sequence of interruptions and adjustments, not a simple story of a craft remaining unchanged until the present.`,
   `Preservation reporting from Nhan Dan follows families who collected blocks from households leaving the profession and continued teaching younger relatives. A block can remain physically intact while the knowledge of using it becomes harder to maintain. Conversely, keeping a tool in use can connect care for an object with care for a working practice.`,
   `Imagine following one design through that history. The design may stay recognizable, yet the workshop, its buyers, the pace of production, and the reasons for making it can all change. Looking only at the finished picture would leave much of that history out.`,
   `Older reports sometimes give figures for families or lineages still practicing. This exhibition does not turn those figures into a current census. Dates and categories matter: a lineage is not the same thing as a household, and an observation from an earlier year is not automatically a statement about today.`
  ],reflection:'What does keeping a craft alive require beyond preserving its tools?',action:{label:'Meet the families carrying it forward',chapter:4}
 },
 {
  id:'families',period:'Generations · April 2024',short:'The makers',title:'Families carrying the blocks forward',image:'gallery',imageNote:'A reconstructed display of study prints. It does not depict the actual 2024 gallery.',sources:['gallery','families','revival'],kind:'Named makers · events dated to their reports',
  intro:`The history becomes more tangible when we follow the people doing the work. Reports describe families connected with artisans Nguyen Dang Che and Nguyen Huu Sam conserving blocks, making prints, and passing on knowledge. In April 2024, Vietnam News Agency reported the opening of a private Dong Ho gallery owned by Nguyen Dang Che. A gallery can show visitors the result; the workshop behind it reminds us that preservation also depends on people who can still make the next impression.`,
  deeper:[
   `The April 2024 Vietnam News Agency report places a specific public event within this longer story: the inauguration of Nguyen Dang Che's private gallery. Its value here is not a claim that one opening solved the difficulties facing the whole craft. It is a documented example of a family creating another way for people to encounter the paintings.`,
   `Nhan Dan's May 2024 reporting describes Che as part of a long family lineage and discusses collecting, conserving, recreating older paintings, and making new work. Earlier preservation reporting also follows Nguyen Huu Qua and the continuation of knowledge associated with his father, Nguyen Huu Sam. These accounts give different kinds of continuity a human scale.`,
   `When you visit an exhibition, it is easy to remember a title and forget the maker. Try reversing that habit. Ask who prepared the surface, carved the shapes, judged the colors, cared for the blocks, or taught someone else to print. A tradition is sustained by many forms of work. This digital studio introduces some of those questions, but its study illustrations and optional drawing guide do not replace the experience or authority of practicing artisans.`
  ],reflection:'Whose work becomes visible when you look beyond the finished picture?',action:{label:'Make something, with a little guidance',href:'WorkshopPage.html',mode:'free'}
 },
 {
  id:'safeguarding',period:'2012 · 2025 · onward',short:'The next chapter',title:'Recognition—and the work ahead',image:'future',imageNote:'An original teaching illustration of continuing practice, not an official UNESCO emblem.',sources:['unesco','government','certificate'],kind:'Verified milestones · continuing responsibility',
  intro:`Recognition gives the craft a public platform, but it is not the end of its story. UNESCO's 2025 decision records national inventory inclusion in 2012 and inscribes Dong Ho woodblock printing on the List of Intangible Cultural Heritage in Need of Urgent Safeguarding. The wording matters: it recognizes a living practice that needs support. Training, livelihoods, access to materials, and the involvement of the community remain central to what happens after the announcement, not simply before it.`,
  deeper:[
   `Read the milestones separately. National inventory inclusion in 2012 belongs to Vietnam's safeguarding framework. The international inscription took place in 2025; Vietnam Government News dates the decision to 9 December. A later certificate ceremony, reported by Bac Ninh province in March 2026, is another event. Receiving a certificate in 2026 does not move the inscription itself into a different year.`,
   `The UNESCO decision identifies pressure on skilled practice and transmission, and supports safeguarding work centered on the community. This makes the designation a prompt for continued attention rather than a guarantee that the practical challenges have disappeared. The official list is an intangible-heritage safeguarding list, not a World Heritage Site designation for a building or landscape.`,
   `What can a visitor take from that distinction? Look for the maker as well as the image. Notice the difference between a traditional print, an archival reproduction, and a new work inspired by the tradition. Learn the process, ask careful questions, and recognize the limits of a digital demonstration. Your own drawing can become a personal response to what you have discovered. It is a contemporary creation, while the people practicing the inherited craft remain at the heart of its future.`
  ],reflection:'How could your curiosity become continued attention to the people behind the craft?',action:{label:'Create your own response',href:'WorkshopPage.html',mode:'free'}
 }
];
